# CWS extension quality (Chromium store)

Official: [Chrome Web Store best practices](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/webstore/best-practices).
Full checklists: `.cursor/skills/chrome-web-store-best-practices/`. Firefox/AMO is separate.

## Hard constraints for this repo

- Stay **MV3** (service worker background). No MV2 persistent background.
- **Privacy-first**: no analytics, tracking, or crash reporters. Keep behavior
  aligned with `PRIVACY.md` and CWS Privacy tab answers.
- Request only permissions needed for inject-by-URL-pattern. Justify
  `<all_urls>` honestly — do not widen “just in case.”
- Prefer HTTPS for extension-initiated network; user rule fetches are
  user-directed and must stay disclosed as such.

## bfcache (do not regress)

In extension page/content scripts (`public/scripts/inject.js` and anything
injected by the extension itself):

- Never add `unload` / `beforeunload` — use `pagehide` or `tabs.onRemoved`.
- Never open WebSocket/WebRTC from content scripts — use the service worker +
  `runtime.connect` if ever required.

Current inject path is fine (message + `load` only). User-authored rule code
may still break bfcache; that is not an extension-code defect unless we add
those APIs ourselves.

## Quality bar before store-bound changes

- Keep Playwright e2e meaningful (`npm run test:e2e`) for inject/popup/options.
- Permission changes: verify install/update warning UI on a packed build.
- If data flows change, update `PRIVACY.md` in the same change.

## UX

- Permissions: optional / `activeTab` only when the feature truly does not need
  standing host access; core matching inject may still need broad hosts.
- Persistent UI should stay low-distraction and on-purpose.
